<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Revolt</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cantonal Rebellion</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1873">1873</a>–<a href="/years/1874">1874</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Cantonal Revolution</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cantonal Rebellion was an uprising of intransigent federal republicans in southern and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> during the First Spanish Republic. From July 1873 cities such as Cartagena, Cádiz, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> proclaimed themselves sovereign cantons. Government troops under Pavía and Martínez Campos put down most of them within weeks, though Cartagena held out until January 1874, weakening the republic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cantonal-rebellion.jpg" alt="Cantonal Rebellion" width="320" height="281" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tyk; derivative work by Durero · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rebeli%C3%B3n_cantonal.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution-spain">Glorious Revolution</a> of 1868 overthrew Isabella II. Amadeo of Savoy, elected king in 1870, faced the assassination of Joan Prim and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-carlist-war">Third Carlist War</a> from 1872, and abdicated on 11 February 1873, when the First Spanish Republic was formed. The Federal Democratic Republican Party, which won the 1873 election, was split between benevolents, who sought a federal republic by legal means, and intransigents, who wanted it built from below by revolution. Prime Minister Francesc Pi i Margall, the founder of the federalist movement, sought conciliation, but the intransigents rose to found cantons on the Swiss model.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In July 1873 intransigents in Cartagena seized the city council and proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a> of Cartagena under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-contreras-y-roman">Juan Contreras y Román</a> and Roque Barcia, taking the warships in its port, most of the Spanish Navy. Pi resigned on 18 July and was replaced by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicolas-salmeron">Nicolás Salmerón</a>, and over the following days cantonalists took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeciras">Algeciras</a>, Cádiz, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salamanca">Salamanca</a>, València and other towns, chiefly in the south and east, from which troops had been withdrawn to pacify the Carlist rebellion. Members of the Spanish Regional Federation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/international-workingmens-association">International Workingmen's Association</a> joined, hoping to turn the rising into a social revolution.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-pavia-y-rodriguez-de-alburquerque">Manuel Pavía</a> led 3,000 soldiers to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, which he took after two days of fighting, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arsenio-martinez-campos">Arsenio Martínez Campos</a> subdued the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> after two weeks. By mid-September 1873 only Cartagena, protected by its walls and naval base, still held out; one of its leaders appealed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> for protection before military officers in the city surrendered.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The rising strengthened the army's hold on politics. Salmerón gave way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emilio-castelar">Emilio Castelar</a>, and on 3 January 1874 Pavía deposed Castelar in a coup and installed Francisco Serrano; within a year Martínez Campos led another coup that restored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbon</a> monarchy under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-xii">Alfonso XII</a>. The anarchist newspaper El Orden reported that prison authorities had drowned 66 participants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Canton</a> of Cádiz, whose mayor Fermín Salvochea was sentenced to life imprisonment. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federalism">Federalism</a> declined, and many federalists turned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anarchism">anarchism</a>, which grew in influence in the late 19th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cantonal Rebellion” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In July 1873 the federalists of Cádiz, a city with strong radical and republican traditions, declared a sovereign canton as part of the <b>Cantonal Rebellion</b> against the central government of the Spanish First Republic.</blockquote><a href="/state/canton-of-cadiz">Canton of Cádiz</a></li><li><blockquote>On 20 July 1873 the federalists of Granada proclaimed a sovereign canton, part of the <b>Cantonal Rebellion</b> that swept southern and eastern Spain against the government of the Spanish First Republic.</blockquote><a href="/state/canton-of-granada">Canton of Granada</a></li><li><blockquote>The government faced the Third Carlist War, the Cuban Ten Years' War and the <b>Cantonal Rebellion</b>, and its presidents, Figueras, Pi, Nicolás Salmerón and Emilio Castelar, followed in quick succession.</blockquote><a href="/state/spanish-first-republic">Spanish First Republic</a></li></ul></section>
